               healthy young adults (n = 991), we combine the whole-brain
               resting state functional magnetic resonance imaging (fMRI) data
               with a mean-filed model on the structural network derived from
               diffusion tensor imaging and demonstrate that resting brain
               networks are on average close to a balanced state. This state
               allows for a balanced time dwelling at segregated and integrated
               configurations and highly flexible switching between them.
               Furthermore, we employ structural equation modeling to estimate
               general and domain-specific cognitive phenotypes from nine tasks
               and demonstrate that network segregation, integration, and their
               balance in resting brains predict individual differences in
               diverse cognitive phenotypes. More specifically, stronger
               integration is associated with better general cognitive ability,
