  publisher = "Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers (IEEE)",
  volume    =  28,
  number    =  5,
  pages     = "1117--1127",
  abstract  = "Transfer learning makes use of data or knowledge in one problem
               to help solve a different, yet related, problem. It is
               particularly useful in brain-computer interfaces (BCIs), for
               coping with variations among different subjects and/or tasks.
               This paper considers offline unsupervised cross-subject
               electroencephalogram (EEG) classification, i.e., we have labeled
               EEG trials from one or more source subjects, but only unlabeled
               EEG trials from the target subject. We propose a novel manifold
               embedded knowledge transfer (MEKT) approach, which first aligns
               the covariance matrices of the EEG trials in the Riemannian
               manifold, extracts features in the tangent space, and then
               performs domain adaptation by minimizing the joint probability
               distribution shift between the source and the target domains,
               while preserving their geometric structures. MEKT can cope with
               one or multiple source domains, and can be computed efficiently.
               We also propose a domain transferability estimation (DTE)
               approach to identify the most beneficial source domains, in case
               there are a large number of source domains. Experiments on four
               EEG datasets from two different BCI paradigms demonstrated that
               MEKT outperformed several state-of-the-art transfer learning
               approaches, and DTE can reduce more than half of the
