               coupling (SFC) can provide more information about the underlying
               workings of the brain than either feature alone. We begin by
               defining SFC and describing the computational methods used to
               quantify it. We then review empirical studies that examine the
               heterogeneous expression of SFC across different brain regions,
               among individuals, in the context of the cognitive task being
               performed, and over time, as well as its role in fostering
               flexible cognition. Last, we investigate how the coupling between
               structure and function is affected in neurological and
               psychiatric conditions, and we report how aberrant SFC is
               associated with disease duration and disease-specific cognitive
               impairment. By elucidating how the dynamic relationship between
